<description>
This PR removes direct access to process-wide state from `ExternalSignerScriptPubKeyMan`.

Instead of reading `gArgs`/`Params()` from the `SPKM`, the wallet load/setup path now reads the external signer command and chain once, stores them on `CWallet`, and passes the narrow values into the external signer SPKM.

This keeps argument and chain selection at the wallet boundary and makes the SPKM less coupled to global state.
</description>

<commits>
96bf425361 wallet: avoid global access in external signer SPKM

ExternalSignerScriptPubKeyMan::GetExternalSigner() reached into global state
(gArgs for -signer, Params() for the chain type) from deep inside the signing
and descriptor-setup paths.

Capture both in LoadWalletArgs -- where the wallet already reads its other
settings from the injected WalletContext -- store them on CWallet, and pass
them to ExternalSignerScriptPubKeyMan through its LoadFromStorage() and
CreateNew() factories. GetExternalSigner() now takes the command and chain as
parameters.

When -signer is unset the command is empty and the same "restart bitcoind
with -signer" error is returned. The chain string comes from the same
ArgsManager that Params() is selected from at startup.

-signer is now read when the wallet is loaded rather than each time a signer
is used, so a value changed at runtime (e.g. via the GUI options dialog,
which already requires a restart) only applies to wallets loaded afterwards.
This matches the other wallet options read in LoadWalletArgs.

d28c185d27 test: cover external signer command and chain

Add unit tests asserting that:
- GetExternalSigner() with an empty command returns the "needs -signer"
  error before any external process is launched.
- LoadWalletArgs captures -signer and the chain from the wallet's
  injected ArgsManager. The chain is set to differ from the global
  Params(), so reading it from globals would fail the test.

In wallet_signer.py, check the debug log when spending from a wallet
loaded with a disconnected signer. A missing signer and an empty -signer
command both surface as the same "External signer not found" RPC error,
so the log message is what shows that the configured command reached
the loaded wallet's ScriptPubKeyMan.
</commits>

